    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q28-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that for any positive integer $n$ there exists a multiple of $n$ that consists only of 1s and 0s.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Two of $1,11,111,\\ldots$ with up to $n+1$ digits have the same remainder modulo $n$.\n\nTheir positive difference is a multiple of $n$ consisting of 1s followed by 0s.",
          "worked": "Let $R_k$ be the integer consisting of $k$ digits, all 1. Consider $R_1,\\ldots,R_{n+1}$. There are only $n$ possible remainders modulo $n$, so the pigeonhole principle gives $R_i\\equiv R_j\\pmod n$ for some $i<j$.\n$$\\begin{align*}R_j-R_i&\\equiv0\\pmod n\\\\R_j-R_i&=10^iR_{j-i}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis positive difference consists of $j-i$ digits equal to 1 followed by $i$ digits equal to 0. It is the required multiple of $n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q28-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that if $n$ is coprime to 2 and 5, then there exists a multiple of $n$ that consists only of 1s.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "From part (a), $n\\mid10^iR$ for an integer $R$ consisting only of 1s.\n\nSince $\\gcd(n,10^i)=1$, it follows that $n\\mid R$.",
          "worked": "By part (a), there is a positive multiple of $n$ of the form $10^iR$, where $R$ consists only of 1s.\nSince $n$ is coprime to 2 and 5, it is coprime to $10^i$. Hence $n\\mid10^iR$ gives $n\\mid R$. Thus $R$ is a multiple of $n$ consisting only of 1s.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      }
    ]
  },
